{"id":"h081","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-physics","question":"How much energy in electron volts must be expended to separate the atoms in the potassium iodide molecule, 39_k 127_I, if the ions are originally separated by a distance R = 3.04 × 10^\\rule{1em}{1pt}10 m? The potential energy for coulomb's law of force is, E_P = \\rule{1em}{1pt}ke^2 / d where e is the electric charge of each ion and d is the distance between the charges. K = 9 × 10^9 N \\textbullet m^2 / c^2\nIs the correct answer \"5.7 eV\"?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The correct answer is about 4.74 eV, not 5.7 eV.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"A positive energy value is expected because the question asks for energy needed to separate the ions.","confidence":0.8,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.2566,"phi":0.0},{"text":"5.7 eV is of the right order of magnitude for an ionic bond, so it is not implausible on its face.","confidence":0.5,"lean":0.3,"theta":0.7854,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The Coulomb energy at separation R is given by U = ke²/R with ion charges ±e.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-0.3,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"With the given values, ke²/R is about 7.6 × 10^-19 J.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-0.5,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Converting to electron volts gives about 4.74 eV.","confidence":0.92,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4451,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Getting 5.7 eV would require a separation of about 2.5 × 10^-10 m, not the stated 3.04 × 10^-10 m.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The direct calculation with the given k, e, and R decides the question and does not match 5.7 eV.","confidence":0.92,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4451,"phi":-3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":2,"J":-0.7,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,